message.content:
You are working in C:\Users\edmem\kodely-prod (Next.js 16.3.1, React 19, Tailwind v4, TypeScript strict; framer-motion + lucide-react available; NO new dependencies).

TASK: Build the **template picker as a modal with a two-stage flow**, as a self-contained component another agent will mount.

READ FIRST:
1. `docs/design-system.md` — binding contract.
2. `components/ui/*` — especially `Modal`, `SearchInput`, `Segmented`, `Badge`, `Button`, `EmptyState`, `Field`. Import them; never hand-roll a second version.
3. `lib/templates.ts` — 23 templates in 7 categories. Each is `{ id, name, description, category, prompt }`. **There is no thumbnail, no preview image, no layout and no starting HTML** — every project builds from the same `FOUNDATION_FILES`.
4. `lib/assets/**` — the in-repo asset catalogue (502 entries: icons, gradients, backdrops, patterns, illustrations, marks). `lib/assets/index.ts` and `lib/assets/illustrations.ts` are the relevant ones. **Everything here is inline SVG or CSS, generated in-house, zero network.**
5. `lib/brand-kit.ts` — how a brand palette is represented and stored.
6. `components/wizard/PromptWizard.tsx` and `components/wizard/LookPicker.tsx` — existing prompt-assembly and colour-picking work. **READ but do NOT edit** — another agent owns that directory's new work. Reuse ideas, copy code into your own files if useful.

## YOU OWN
`components/templates/TemplatePickerModal.tsx` (new) and any new files you add under `components/templates/`. Do NOT edit `components/templates/TemplateCard.tsx` or `TemplateGallery.tsx` (the public `/templates` page uses them). Do NOT touch `app/**`, `components/ui/**`, `components/wizard/**`, or `lib/**`.

## THE EXACT INTERFACE — another agent mounts this, so it must match

```tsx
export type TemplatePickerResult = {
  templateId: string;
  /** The finished, editable build prompt with all answers substituted in. */
  prompt: string;
  /** Data URL, only if the customer uploaded one. */
  logoDataUrl?: string;
  /** Hex strings the customer chose, in the order they should be applied. */
  palette?: string[];
};

export function TemplatePickerModal(props: {
  open: boolean;
  onClose: () => void;
  onComplete: (result: TemplatePickerResult) => void;
}): React.ReactNode;
```

A stub with this exact signature already exists at that path. Replace it.

## STAGE 1 — Gallery

Inside the modal: category filter, a search bar (searching name, description AND prompt body), and a card per template.

**Each card needs a thumbnail, and there is no image to use.** Do not fetch anything and do not ship a broken `<img>`. Generate a distinctive cover per template from the in-repo catalogue — `lib/assets` has backdrops, gradients, patterns, spot illustrations and 147 icons, all inline SVG driven by a seed. Make the covers *look like the kind of site the template builds* (a restaurant template should not look like a SaaS one) and make them visually distinct from each other. This is a real design problem; spend effort on it. Deterministic, so a template always looks the same.

## STAGE 2 — Intake

Once a template is chosen, ask the questions that template actually needs, then substitute the answers into its prompt.

- **The questions must be derived per template, not one generic list.** A restaurant needs name, city, cuisine, opening hours; a photographer portfolio needs name, speciality, location. Read all 23 prompts in `lib/templates.ts` and work out what each genuinely needs. Where a prompt already contains a placeholder-shaped phrase, drive the field from it.
- Logo upload — image only, downscaled client-side to a data URL. Follow the approach in `app/(portal)/dashboard/new/image.ts` (read it): narrow the picker to png/jpg/webp and re-encode, so the output always matches the `data:image/(png|jpeg|webp);base64,` regex the generate route enforces. Reject anything else in the UI with a reason.
- Colour palette picker. `lib/brand-kit.ts` and `LookPicker.tsx` show how colour is already handled — stay consistent with that vocabulary rather than inventing a second one.
- Every field must be **optional and skippable**, with the prompt degrading gracefully — someone who wants to just start must be able to. Show the assembled prompt before they commit, editable.
- Back to the gallery without losing what they typed.

## HARD RULES
- **Never build something that only pretends to work.** No fake screenshots, no stock photos, no remote image URLs.
- Accessible: the modal already traps focus (native `<dialog>`); your stages must manage heading order and move focus sensibly on stage change.
- `k-focus` on every interactive element. Works in light AND dark theme — use token classes, never raw hex except in the palette picker's own swatches.
- Must pass `npx tsc --noEmit` and `npx eslint app lib components --max-warnings=0`, zero errors and zero warnings.

REPORT BACK: your cover-generation approach and why it makes 23 distinguishable covers, how you derived per-template questions (and any template where that was hard), what you made optional, and anything untested.

